-- title:
--   Purely atomic Bayesian game
-- statement:
--   Let $\Omega$ be a measurable space of states, $I$ a set of players and $A_i$ the action set of player $i$. A **purely atomic Bayesian game** consists of
--
--   1. for each player $i$ a **type function** $t^i:\Omega\to\Delta(\Omega)$, $\omega\mapsto t^i_\omega$, where each $t^i_\omega$ is a probability measure, $\omega\mapsto t^i_\omega(S)$ is Borel for every Borel $S$, and $t^i_\omega(S)=1$ whenever $\omega\in S$ and $S$ is a Borel set saturated for player $i$'s **knowledge relation** $\omega\,E_i\,\omega' \iff t^i_\omega=t^i_{\omega'}$;
--   2. **purely atomic** knowledge: every class of $E_i$ is countable;
--   3. for each player $i$ a **prior** $\mu^i$: a probability measure of which $t^i$ is a proper regular conditional distribution, i.e. $\mu^i(S)=\int_\Omega t^i_\omega(S)\,d\mu^i(\omega)$ for every Borel $S$;
--   4. a **payoff** $r^i(\omega,a)\in\mathbb R$ for each player $i$, state $\omega$ and pure action profile $a\in\prod_j A_j$, Borel in $\omega$ and bounded.
--
--   Each player may have their own prior; no common prior is assumed. This is the object of the paper's main theorem.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The knowledge relations are not separate data: the paper defines $E_i$ from the types (p.11), so $\sigma(E_i)$-measurability of $t^i$ is plain Borel measurability. The second clause of the proper-RCD condition (2.2) and the purely atomic types of Definition 2.9 follow from item 1. $\Delta(\Omega)$ carries the σ-algebra generated by the evaluations $\nu\mapsto\nu(S)$, which for Polish $\Omega$ is the Borel σ-algebra of the weak* topology.
-- source:
--   Hellman–Levy, Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence Relation Hierarchy, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), §2.5, p.9, eq. (2.2); §2.6, pp.10–12, Definitions 2.8–2.9; §3.1, p.12; §4.1, p.16

import Mathlib

namespace HellmanLevy.Bayes

/-- A **purely atomic Bayesian game** `B = (Ω, I, t, (µⁱ), A, r)` on the state space `Ω`, with
players `I` and action sets `A i`:

* `t i : Ω → ∆(Ω)` is player `i`'s **type function**; `t i ω` is a probability measure, `t i` is
  Borel (`ω ↦ tⁱ_ω(S)` is Borel for each Borel `S`), and `tⁱ_ω(S) = 1` whenever `ω ∈ S` and `S` is
  a Borel set saturated for player `i`'s knowledge relation `ω Eᵢ ω' ↔ tⁱ_ω = tⁱ_ω'`;
* **purely atomic**: every class of `Eᵢ` is countable;
* `µ i` is a **prior** of player `i`: a probability measure of which `t i` is a proper regular
  conditional distribution given `σ(Eᵢ)`, i.e. `µⁱ(S) = ∫ tⁱ_ω(S) dµⁱ(ω)` for all Borel `S`;
* `r i ω a` is player `i`'s payoff at state `ω` under the pure profile `a`; it is Borel in `ω`
  and bounded.

Hellman–Levy, *Equilibria Existence in Bayesian Games: Climbing the Countable Borel Equivalence
Relation Hierarchy*, author's manuscript (2020-07-14), §2.5, p.9, eq. (2.2) (proper RCD); §2.6, pp.10–12 (knowledge spaces, type functions,
Definitions 2.8–2.9, priors); §3.1, p.12 (Bayesian games; "From here, we will presume a purely
atomic Bayesian game"); §4.1, p.16 ("assume each player i has a prior µⁱ").

**Formalization Note** The knowledge relations are not separate data: "A type space implicitly
defines the knowledge relations Eᵢ underlying the type functions: ωEᵢω′ … if and only if
tⁱ_ω = tⁱ_ω′" (p.11). With this, `σ(Eᵢ)`-measurability of `tⁱ` (fn. 10) is Borel measurability
(`measurable_t`), and Definition 2.8 is `countable_class`. The second clause of (2.2)
(`tω(A) = 1` for µ-a.e. `ω ∈ A ∈ F`) follows from `knows`; purely atomic *types*
(Definition 2.9) follow from `knows` applied to the (Borel, saturated) countable class of `ω`.
The space `∆(Ω)` carries the Giry σ-algebra (generated by the evaluations `ν ↦ ν(S)`), which for
Polish `Ω` is the Borel σ-algebra of the weak* topology used on p.6. The action sets are finite
(and, in the theorems, non-empty); payoffs are given on pure profiles and extend multilinearly
(see `mixedPayoff`). Each player has their own prior; no common prior is assumed. -/
structure BayesianGame (Ω : Type*) [MeasurableSpace Ω] (I : Type*) (A : I → Type*) where
  /-- The type functions `tⁱ : Ω → ∆(Ω)`. -/
  t : I → Ω → MeasureTheory.Measure Ω
  /-- The priors `µⁱ`. -/
  μ : I → MeasureTheory.Measure Ω
  /-- The payoffs `rⁱ(ω, a)` on pure action profiles. -/
  r : I → Ω → (∀ i, A i) → ℝ
  isProb_t : ∀ i ω, MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure (t i ω)
  measurable_t : ∀ i, Measurable (t i)
  knows : ∀ i (S : Set Ω), MeasurableSet S → (∀ x y, t i x = t i y → (x ∈ S ↔ y ∈ S)) →
    ∀ ω ∈ S, t i ω S = 1
  countable_class : ∀ i ω, {ω' | t i ω' = t i ω}.Countable
  isProb_μ : ∀ i, MeasureTheory.IsProbabilityMeasure (μ i)
  prior : ∀ i, (μ i).bind (t i) = μ i
  measurable_r : ∀ i a, Measurable fun ω => r i ω a
  bounded_r : ∃ M : ℝ, ∀ i ω a, |r i ω a| ≤ M

end HellmanLevy.Bayes


